# OllaVoice — local speech input and speech output for Ollama

Ollama is text in, text out. It has no native microphone support and no native
text-to-speech — the [feature request is still open](https://github.com/ollama/ollama/issues/11021)
and the [official API](https://docs.ollama.com/api) has no audio endpoints as of
v0.40.x. **OllaVoice is the missing layer**: a native desktop app that sits on top
of your existing Ollama install and gives it ears and a voice.

```
   you ──▶ microphone ──▶ Whisper (local)  ─┐
                                            ├─▶ Ollama (local) ─▶ Piper/Kokoro (local) ─▶ speakers
   keyboard ────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Nothing leaves your machine.** No cloud API, no telemetry, no account, no
licence key. Audio, transcription, generation and synthesis all run on your own
hardware. Once setup has downloaded the models you can pull the network cable and
it keeps working — the code refuses to phone home even to check for model updates.

---

## What you get

| | |
|---|---|
| **Native desktop GUI** | Qt 6 app: chat transcript, push-to-talk mic button with a live level meter, model/voice pickers in the toolbar, dark theme, system tray, global hotkeys |
| **Speech input** | OpenAI Whisper via `faster-whisper` (int8 on CPU, fp16 on CUDA). Optional: your own `whisper.cpp` build, or the recogniser built into Windows |
| **Speech output** | **Piper** (fast, ~60 MB per voice, 100+ voices/languages) and **Kokoro-82M** (more natural, ~140 MB), switchable live. Falls back to the voices already installed in Windows |
| **Sentence streaming** | The answer is spoken sentence-by-sentence *while* Ollama is still generating, so you hear audio in about a second instead of waiting for the whole reply |
| **Terminal mode** | `ollavoice chat` — the same pipeline as a REPL, for SSH boxes and people who live in a terminal |
| **Local HTTP API** | OpenAI-compatible `/v1/audio/transcriptions` and `/v1/audio/speech`, plus `/api/chat` for speech-in/speech-out. Loopback only |
| **Browser UI** | Served by that same local server, for when you want a second window |
| **Hands-free mode** | Optional adaptive VAD that ends your turn when you stop talking |
| **Air-gapped installs** | `ollavoice bundle` makes a portable model folder; `setup --offline` installs from it |

---

## Quick start (Windows)

1. Install [Ollama for Windows](https://ollama.com/download/windows) and pull a
   model: `ollama pull llama3.2:3b`
2. Install [Python 3.10 – 3.13](https://www.python.org/downloads/windows/)
   (tick *Add python.exe to PATH*)
3. Double-click **`install-windows.bat`**

That creates `.venv`, installs the packages, detects your hardware, picks a
performance tier, downloads the speech models, verifies the whole loop by
synthesising a sentence and transcribing it back, and adds Start Menu shortcuts.

Then press **Start → OllaVoice**, hold **Space**, and talk.

<details>
<summary>Prefer to do it by hand?</summary>

```powershell
cd ollavoice
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
pip install -e ".[all]"        # or: pip install -r requirements-windows.txt
ollavoice setup                # hardware detection + model downloads + self-test
ollavoice doctor --test        # prove the loop works
ollavoice gui                  # launch the desktop app
```
</details>

Linux and macOS: `./scripts/setup.sh` (see [docs/INSTALL-windows.md](docs/INSTALL-windows.md)
for the full Windows guide, including offline/air-gapped installs and building a
standalone `.exe`).

---

## Using it

### Desktop app

| Action | How |
|---|---|
| Talk | Hold **Space**, or hold the mic button, or **Ctrl+Alt+Space** anywhere in Windows |
| Send | Release the key/button |
| Type instead | Click the text box, press Enter |
| Stop the voice | **Esc**, the Stop button, or **Ctrl+Alt+X** anywhere |
| New conversation | **Ctrl+N** |
| Read the last answer again | **Ctrl+R** |
| Settings | **Ctrl+,** |
| Transcribe a recording | File → Transcribe an audio file |

The global hotkeys use `RegisterHotKey`, so push-to-talk works even when
OllaVoice is in the background — the tray icon stays resident and the window can
be closed without quitting.

### Terminal

```powershell
ollavoice chat                 # hold Enter to record, Enter again to send
ollavoice chat --hands-free    # auto-detect end of speech
ollavoice chat -p "explain what a GGUF file is"
```

Inside the REPL: `/help`, `/voice`, `/model qwen3:8b`, `/tts kokoro`,
`/rate 1.2`, `/lang de`, `/voices`, `/new`, `/save notes.txt`, `/doctor`, `/gui`.

### One-shot commands

```powershell
ollavoice listen                                   # mic -> transcript on stdout
ollavoice listen -d 5 --save-audio take.wav        # fixed 5 second recording
ollavoice transcribe meeting.wav --timestamps -o meeting.txt
ollavoice speak "Read this aloud" 
ollavoice speak -f notes.md -o notes.wav --engine kokoro --kokoro-voice af_heart
ollavoice voices --all --language de               # browse the Piper catalogue
ollavoice voices --install de_DE-thorsten-high
ollavoice serve                                    # local API + browser UI
ollavoice doctor --test                            # full self-test
ollavoice bundle D:\ollavoice-bundle               # offline bundle for another PC
```

### Local HTTP API

`ollavoice serve` (or Settings → Advanced → *Run the local speech API*) exposes:

```http
POST /v1/audio/transcriptions     multipart: file, model?, language?, response_format?
POST /v1/audio/speech             {"input": "...", "voice": "...", "speed": 1.0}  -> audio/wav
POST /api/chat                    {"text": "..."} or {"audio_b64": "..."}         -> {"text", "audio_b64"}
GET  /health  /config  /voices    status
GET  /                            browser UI with a push-to-talk button
```

The first two are OpenAI-shaped, so existing tooling can point at
`http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1` instead of a cloud provider:

```powershell
curl.exe http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1/audio/transcriptions -F "file=@take.wav"
curl.exe http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1/audio/speech -H "Content-Type: application/json" `
       -d '{\"input\":\"Hello from localhost\",\"voice\":\"en_GB-alan-medium\"}' -o hello.wav
```

It binds to loopback and has **no authentication** — do not expose it to a
network you do not trust.

---

## Performance tiers

`ollavoice setup` measures your CPU, RAM and VRAM and picks a tier. You can
override it any time (`ollavoice config --tier high`, or Settings → General).

| Tier | Ollama model | Whisper | Voice | Speech models | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `low` | `qwen3:4b` | `base.en` | Piper `en_US-lessac-medium` | ~320 MB | CPU-only laptops, no AVX2 |
| `balanced` | `qwen3:8b` | `small.en` | Piper `en_US-lessac-high` | ~700 MB | Default. 8 GB+ RAM or any 6 GB+ GPU |
| `high` | `qwen3:14b` | `medium.en` | Kokoro `af_heart` | ~1.7 GB | 12 GB+ VRAM |

Real numbers from a deliberately crippled test box (2 CPU cores, 1 GB RAM, no
GPU — i.e. much worse than your machine):

| Stage | Result |
|---|---|
| Piper synthesis | 5.9 s of audio in 5.1 s (1.15× realtime) |
| Whisper `base.en` | 5.9 s of audio in 1.6 s (**0.28× realtime**) |
| Kokoro int8 | 4.4 s of audio in 18.6 s (4× realtime — this is why Piper is the default) |

Sentence streaming hides most of the synthesis cost: the first sentence is
usually spoken before the model has finished thinking.

---

## Requirements

* **Ollama** running locally, with at least one model pulled
* **Python 3.10 – 3.13** (64-bit). 3.11/3.12 have the best wheel coverage
* **Windows 10/11**, Linux or macOS. Windows is the primary target
* A microphone and speakers
* Disk: ~1 GB for the balanced tier (plus your Ollama models)
* Optional: an NVIDIA GPU for faster Whisper (`float16` is selected automatically)

Python packages, all with prebuilt Windows wheels (no compiler needed):
`numpy`, `soundfile`, `faster-whisper`, `piper-tts`, `kokoro-onnx`, `PySide6`,
`sounddevice`. The `piper-tts` and `espeakng-loader` wheels bundle espeak-ng and
its phoneme data, so there is nothing to compile or install system-wide.

---

## How it stays offline

* Setup is the **only** network step. Downloads come from Hugging Face
  (Whisper, Piper voices), GitHub releases (Kokoro) and your local Ollama server.
* Once a Whisper model is on disk, `HF_HUB_OFFLINE` is set automatically so
  `huggingface_hub` does not check for revisions on every start.
* `OLLAVOICE_OFFLINE=1` (or `setup --offline`) makes every download call fail
  fast with the exact path to copy the file to, instead of hanging on a blocked
  network.
* `ollavoice bundle <folder>` copies everything you downloaded into a portable
  folder for an air-gapped machine.
* The local API binds to `127.0.0.1`. There is no telemetry, no update check and
  no crash reporting anywhere in the codebase.

To verify for yourself: run it with the network disabled and watch
`ollavoice doctor --test` pass.

---

## Configuration

One JSON file: `%APPDATA%\OllaVoice\config.json`
(models live in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\OllaVoice\models`).

```powershell
ollavoice config show
ollavoice config get tts.piper_voice
ollavoice config set tts.engine kokoro
ollavoice config set tts.kokoro_voice am_michael
ollavoice config set stt.model medium.en
ollavoice config set stt.language de
ollavoice config set vad.enabled true          # hands-free listening
ollavoice config set hotkeys.push_to_talk "Ctrl+Shift+Space"
ollavoice config --tier high
```

Every key is also in the GUI (Settings, five tabs) and documented in
[`ollavoice/config.py`](ollavoice/config.py).

